Southern Rodent Tick

Bio Characteristics: The Southern Rodent tick usually last from 2-3 years, they are small (1-3mm) with an oval body shape and are reddish brown or black uniformly coloured, most active through spring till autumn with the most active months being April and October.

Typical Habitat: Underground burrows of small mammals.

Health Risks: Risk of Lyme disease, bacterial infections and other syndromes from a bite of this tick.


Marsh Tick:

Bio Characteristics: The Marsh Tick often have a lifespan of 1-2 years however it may be extended in colder climates, a bit bigger than other ticks (4-5mm) with an oval body with reddish brown or black legs with a dark brown back with white/silver patterns on their backs, most active during colder months and seasons such as late winter, early spring or autumn with the most active months being February - April.

Typical Habitat: In marshes, fens, swamps and wetlands, usually attached to vegetation.

Health Risks: Risk of Lyme Disease and other dangerous pathogens.















Fox/Badger Tick:

Bio Characteristics: Often has a lifespan of 3 years, going from egg, to larva, to nymph, to adult. They are characterised by a tough, pale, yellowish shell on their back (called the scutum) which is smaller in females - they also have visible mouthparts. Females most commonly attach to the host, then dropping off after 7-8 days to give birth. They most commonly attach to badgers, foxes, or dogs, with human contact being a rarity due to its preference towards damp, sheltered environments. They are active from early spring to late autumn, with peak activity occuring from April to May.

Typical Habitat: Tendency to share a habitat with their host, such as in dog dens/kennels, or fox/badger earths or nests.

Health Risks: Risk of Lyme Disease, or Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever. They require a blood-host at each stage of their lice-cycle (yearly).
